<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Freising</h1><p class="st-meta">1294 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Freising</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Bavarian church state of Saint Corbinian, a small principality on the Isar with scattered estates reaching into Austria and Carniola.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The diocese goes back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corbinian">Saint Corbinian</a>, who worked at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> around 720, and was formally organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> in 739. Over the following centuries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gathered estates across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. Their territory as an imperial principality took shape around the city and the county of Werdenfels, bought in 1294.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, elected by the cathedral chapter, ruled as a prince of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but his small core lands were surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and he depended heavily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> dukes, several of whose sons held the see. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising's</a> cathedral hill was a centre of learning, and the nearby abbey of Weihenstephan brewed beer. The territory remained Catholic.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1802 and 1803 the prince-bishopric was secularised and its lands passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>, while estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The see moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1821 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-munich-and-freising">Archdiocese of Munich and Freising</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-freising.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Freising" width="320" height="288"><figcaption>Image: Roke (map), Hanno Sandvik (addition of colours, based on Putzger Historischer Weltatlas, 103rd ed., 2005) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HRR_1648_Freising.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1294 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Freising</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philipp of the Palatinate, Johann Theodor of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Freising</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-freising" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Freising on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4953 485 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Freising lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5273 741L5229 694M5273 741L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5273 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5273 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Freising. <a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-freising">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
